/** Forms & integrations: the CRM / mailing-list paragraph. */
const crmDoc = (i: ClientRepoInput) =>
	i.outbox && i.formCrm
		? `**CRM and mailing lists.** A new form message can also create or update a contact in **Brevo**, **HubSpot** or **Mailchimp** — as well as the email, never instead of it. An Admin turns a provider on in **Form messages → Settings → CRM & mailing lists**${i.formCrm.providers.length ? ` (this site starts with ${i.formCrm.providers.map((p) => CRM_NAMES[p] ?? p).join(", ")} pre-selected for every form)` : " (everything is off until then)"}, then picks per form which providers get it and which form field is sent as email, first / last / full name, phone, company or message — fields are matched automatically from their names and can be changed. **Only the mapped fields are sent**, never the rest of the message, files or signatures. **Marketing:** when a form has a newsletter / opt-in checkbox mapped, the contact is subscribed only if the visitor ticked it (Brevo: added to the list you name; Mailchimp: subscribed, or asked to confirm with double opt-in; HubSpot: the opt-in property you name is set); without a tick, or without such a checkbox, the contact is only created or updated (Mailchimp: skipped, or added as a transactional contact if you choose so) and an existing subscription is never changed. Sending never slows down or blocks a form; a temporary failure is retried for up to a day, and errors (with the visitor's details removed) show in the same Settings section. A provider is offered only once its secret is set by whoever manages the Cloudflare account: \`npx wrangler secret put BREVO_API_KEY\`${i.formCrm.brevoEmailKey ? " (or nothing: the Brevo email key is reused)" : ""}, \`npx wrangler secret put HUBSPOT_TOKEN\` (private app token, scope \`crm.objects.contacts.write\` only) or \`npx wrangler secret put MAILCHIMP_API_KEY\`. Sites with sensitive data never get this. Setup: \`packages/forms/README.md\` → CRM and mailing lists.

`
		: "";

/** The editor guide's Privacy & consent section. */
const consentGuide = (c: ClientRepoInput["consent"]) =>
	c
		? `
## Privacy and the cookie banner

**Plugins → Privacy & consent** (administrators only) controls the site's own cookie banner:

- **Mode**: *Off* (no banner), *Notice* (US style: everything on until a visitor opts out), *Opt-in* (EU/UK style:
  nothing that stores data on a visitor's device loads until they agree), or *By visitor region* (recommended: opt-in
  for visitors from the EU/EEA, the UK, Switzerland and Québec, a notice for everyone else). This site starts in
  **${c.mode}**${c.keptTools?.length ? ` because the old site's ${c.keptTools.join(", ")} was kept (two banners would ask twice); choosing a mode switches to the built-in banner and stops ${c.keptTools.join(", ")}` : ""}.
- **Wording** (plain text, length-limited), the **privacy policy page** (picked from the site's pages), the
  description of each category and where the banner sits (bottom bar or corner card).
- **What each category covers**: analytics (Google Analytics, Tag Manager, Clarity, Hotjar), marketing (Google Ads,
  Meta Pixel), preferences (chat and review widgets). Cloudflare Web Analytics stores nothing on devices and always
  runs. The list follows the tracking codes in SEO & Analytics → Analytics.
- **Consent log** (off by default): counts per day of accepted / rejected / chosen — no IP, no identifier.
- **Preview the banner** opens the saved banner on the site; **Ask every visitor again** after adding a new kind of
  tracking.

Visitors change their choice with **Cookie settings** at the bottom of every page (any link or button with the
\`data-consent-open\` attribute does the same). Browsers that send Global Privacy Control get marketing off. Google
consent mode v2 starts denied and follows the choice; Meta and Clarity get their consent signals.

For developers: settings in the site's database (\`plugin:privacy-consent:settings\`, read by \`src/utils/consent.ts\`),
the banner in \`public/scripts/site-consent.js\` (choice kept in the visitor's browser: \`site_consent\` cookie and
localStorage), the region answer at \`/_consent/region\` (\`src/middleware.ts\`, never cached). Pages stay one cached
HTML for everyone. Saving purges the cached pages.
`
		: "";

/** The editor guide's Site health section. */
const healthGuide = (h: ClientRepoInput["health"], productionUrl: string) =>
	h
		? `
## Site health and the monthly report

**Plugins → Site health** (administrators only) watches the site around the clock and shows the result in one place:

- **Overall status** at the top — **Healthy**, **Attention** (the site is up but something needs a look) or **Down** —
  and the same on the dashboard.
- **Checks**, each with its last run and the last 14 days: uptime (the home page and a rotating sample of pages every 5
  minutes), whether search engines may index the site, form email (the outbox), broken links (daily; links to other
  sites weekly), the domain's email records (unchanged since go-live), the security certificate, speed (real-visitor
  Core Web Vitals from Google, when set up) and whether the pages still carry the tracking codes switched on in
  SEO & Analytics.
- **Incidents**: when the site or a page failed two checks in a row (5 minutes apart), and when it answered again.
- **Broken links**: the link, its answer and the pages that link to it — fix it in the page editor.
- **Missing pages**: the addresses visitors asked for this week that do not exist, most requested first, each with a
  suggested page to send them to. **Create redirect** on a row sends visitors of that address to the suggested page
  (or to another page of the site you pick) with a permanent redirect, added under **Redirects** where you can change
  or delete it; or fix the link that sent people there. Requests for WordPress login pages and similar bot probes are
  left out.
- **Run checks now** re-checks everything except links (which continue in the background).
- **Lab score (PageSpeed)** on the Speed tab, when your developer added a Google API key: once a week Google tests the
  home page on a simulated phone and a desktop and scores it 0–100 (90+ good), with the trend over 26 weeks and the
  element that loads last. It is a lab test from one place; the real-visitor numbers above it are what visitors
  actually experienced. **Run PageSpeed test now** gives a fresh result within about 5 minutes. You get an email when
  the phone score drops by 15 points or more.

**Coming-soon mode** (Site health → Settings): shows every visitor a holding page with your logo and a message of your
own instead of the site — for example while it is being rebuilt — and tells search engines to come back later, so the
site keeps its place in Google. You still see the site while you are signed in. To show the site to someone without an
admin account (a colleague, a reviewer), send them the **preview link** on the same screen: it works in their browser
for 7 days, and **New link** stops every link sent before. It takes up to a minute to switch; switch it off on the same
screen. To plan it ahead, fill in **Start at** and **End at** (UTC, for example \`2026-12-01 09:00\`): the holding page
shows between those times even with the switch off, and says "Back at …" with the end time. A planned start or end
takes up to 5 minutes to reach every page.

**Page cache** (Site health → Speed): visitors get ready-made pages from Cloudflare's cache. Every edit clears them, and
the site builds them again by itself every 15 minutes, so nobody waits. Choose how often (15, 30 or 60 minutes) or switch
it off there, see what the last refill found, and use **Refresh all pages now** after a big change.

**Security** (Site health → Security): the site tells browsers exactly which scripts and embeds may load, so code
injected into a page cannot run. When a visitor's browser blocks something anyway, it reports what was blocked and on
which page (nothing about the visitor), and it is listed here in plain words — a blocked script usually means something
on that page does not work, so pass the list to your developer; the first time a new script is blocked you also get an
email. **Clear the list** starts over; the switch on the same tab stops collecting. Below it: the security protections
the home page sends (checked every hour) and the certificate.

**Emails.** When the site goes down and comes back, when new broken links appear, and when search-engine access, form
email, the email DNS records, the certificate or the analytics tags start failing, and when a visitor's browser blocks a
new script, an email goes to the alert
recipients${h.alertsTo.length ? ` (by default ${h.alertsTo.join(", ")})` : ""} — at most one per issue per day. On the
1st of each month a **report** for the month before goes to ${h.reportTo.length ? h.reportTo.join(", ") : "the report recipients"} (with a copy to the alert
recipients): availability and outages, speed, visits and the most viewed pages, how many form messages arrived (the
number only, never their content), pages published and updated, broken links found and fixed, and the average SEO
score. Change the recipients, or switch alerts or the report off, under **Site health → Settings**; **Send a test
email** checks that they arrive. Reports are kept under **Reports** for 12 months — **Download** gives a page to print
or save as PDF. Emails start once ${productionUrl} shows this site (after the switch-over) and use the same email
service as the website's forms. The footer reads "${h.footer}".
`
		: "";

/** The editor guide's Backups section (the full guide is docs/backups.md). */
